WEBVTT

00:16.015 --> 00:18.213
Fully eight months on patrol and nothin'.

00:18.338 --> 00:20.716
You know, Rupert, I miss
the Earth so much.

00:20.783 --> 00:21.923
I miss my wife.

00:21.991 --> 00:23.094
It's lonely out in spac...

00:23.163 --> 00:24.837
(beeping)

00:26.881 --> 00:28.657
Uh-oh, Rupert,
we've got company,

00:28.725 --> 00:30.733
and I don't think
it's the Avon lady.

00:32.947 --> 00:34.287
Strap yourself in.

00:34.355 --> 00:35.743
No matter what happens,

00:35.811 --> 00:38.004
it's been a privilege
to serve under you.

00:43.950 --> 00:46.690
(laser weapons firing)

01:14.362 --> 01:15.878
Don't relax yet, Rupert,

01:15.954 --> 01:18.342
those fighters had to have
been coming from somewhere.

01:20.536 --> 01:23.103
Oh, my God.

01:34.500 --> 01:36.220
(mechanical whirring)

01:41.546 --> 01:43.498
(beeping)

01:43.566 --> 01:46.218
Wait a minute, Rupert,

01:46.288 --> 01:47.697
there's a weapons
systems cooling duct

01:47.765 --> 01:49.415
on the far side
of the hull.

01:49.483 --> 01:51.501
If we can get just
one good shot at it,

01:51.570 --> 01:54.125
we can blow that fat
bastard out of the cosmos.

01:54.193 --> 01:56.044
(laser weapon firing)

02:03.146 --> 02:05.030
(laser weapons firing)

02:27.995 --> 02:30.046
(beeping)
Uh-oh, Rupert, our
main blasters are gone.

02:30.113 --> 02:32.531
Looks like this is
a suicide mission.

02:36.970 --> 02:39.929
(beeping continues)

02:39.997 --> 02:41.574
(Stewie yelling)

02:41.641 --> 02:44.801
Got you! Got you! Got you!
Got you! Got you!

02:44.869 --> 02:47.154
Stewie, get lost. Daddy's
trying to watch the ball game.

02:47.221 --> 02:49.404
You can't talk. I've knocked
out your communication systems.

02:49.473 --> 02:50.715
Hey, hey, look, Stewie,

02:50.785 --> 02:52.194
Rupert wants to go play
down in the basement.

02:52.262 --> 02:53.570
(high-pitched voice):
Hey, hey, Stewie,

02:53.638 --> 02:55.152
I'm gonna go play
down in the basement.

02:55.221 --> 02:56.329
Here I go.
в™Є Do-do-do-do-do. в™Є

02:56.395 --> 02:57.704
What? Why, Rupert?

02:57.772 --> 02:58.881
Why do you want to play
in the basement?

02:58.949 --> 03:00.525
I don't know, I just feel like

03:00.594 --> 03:02.004
there might be some
fun stuff down there.

03:02.071 --> 03:03.278
I'm not following
your logic.

03:03.346 --> 03:04.958
And why are you talking
different than usual?

03:05.026 --> 03:06.068
Here I go.

03:06.136 --> 03:07.376
Rupert, get back here.

03:08.518 --> 03:09.828
Rupert?

03:09.898 --> 03:11.240
Oh, there you are.

03:11.308 --> 03:13.927
Wow, look at all
the old stuff down here.

03:13.995 --> 03:15.204
Hello, what's this?

03:18.227 --> 03:20.543
"What I Learned
on Jefferson Street.

03:20.611 --> 03:22.860
Written by H. Brian Griffin."

03:22.928 --> 03:24.741
Oh, yes.

03:32.331 --> 03:33.538
What's this?

03:33.604 --> 03:34.679
What the hell...

03:34.746 --> 03:35.920
Where the hell
did this come from?

03:35.988 --> 03:37.165
What is it, Brian?

03:37.233 --> 03:38.809
It's the first thing
I ever wrote.

03:38.877 --> 03:40.724
It's a script for a
television show, a drama.

03:40.791 --> 03:42.571
I had an idea for a script once.

03:42.638 --> 03:44.788
<i>It's basically Jaws, except
when the guys in the boat</i>

03:44.856 --> 03:46.299
are going after Jaws,

03:46.367 --> 03:48.583
they look around and there's
an even bigger Jaws,

03:48.650 --> 03:52.008
so the guys have to team up
with Jaws to get bigger Jaws.

03:52.074 --> 03:53.754
<i>I call it Big Jaws.</i>

03:53.821 --> 03:55.600
God, I haven't looked
at this in years.

03:55.667 --> 03:58.050
I don't even remember
if it's any good or not.

03:58.118 --> 04:00.234
Oh, wait a minute.

04:00.303 --> 04:02.351
Yeah, yeah, this is all
coming back to me.

04:02.419 --> 04:05.338
You know, this is actually
not too bad a piece of work.

04:05.405 --> 04:07.889
This was... this was kind
of deep as I recall.

04:07.957 --> 04:10.004
You know, Lois, you-you
might actually be interested

04:10.073 --> 04:12.186
in reading this.

04:12.254 --> 04:13.831
You-you...
you might actually...

04:13.900 --> 04:16.417
You-you might like this.

04:16.485 --> 04:18.364
You should look at it.

04:24.541 --> 04:26.519
All right, damn it!

04:29.469 --> 04:31.947
So, have you read
the script yet?

04:32.016 --> 04:34.662
Oh, I'm sorry, Brian,
I haven't gotten around to it.

04:34.731 --> 04:36.103
Why not now, you know?

04:36.170 --> 04:37.310
Just get it
off your plate.

04:37.377 --> 04:38.885
Oh, gosh, you know what?

04:38.953 --> 04:40.895
I don't even know what I did
with the copy that you ga...

04:40.964 --> 04:42.778
Oh, (bleep) there it is.

04:42.847 --> 04:44.320
Hey, you know what else
would be great?

04:44.388 --> 04:46.397
Why-why don't you put check
marks next to scenes you like?

04:46.466 --> 04:48.540
Well, I don't have a...
Here's a pen.

04:48.609 --> 04:51.154
You know, I'll-I'll just
leave it for you right here.

04:51.223 --> 04:53.733
Okay. Thank you, Brian.

04:53.802 --> 04:55.676
Hey, you know what I'll do
to make it even easier?

04:55.744 --> 04:57.820
I'll just go ahead and
place this in your hand.

04:57.889 --> 04:59.261
There we are.

04:59.329 --> 05:01.641
And now when you go, "Oh,
where's that crazy pen?"

05:01.709 --> 05:03.048
Well, there it is.

05:03.115 --> 05:04.488
It's in your hand.

05:04.556 --> 05:06.868
Brian, I'm not gonna read this
with you standing here.

05:06.935 --> 05:08.946
Oh, it's okay,
I don't mind.

05:09.014 --> 05:10.521
Go for a ride in the car?

05:10.589 --> 05:11.626
(gasps)

05:15.347 --> 05:16.553
Come on!

05:20.711 --> 05:23.825
Why the hell's it taking her
so long to read that thing?

05:23.893 --> 05:25.902
I don't know, I saw
her giving Joe notes

05:25.971 --> 05:27.040
on his pilot this morning.

05:27.109 --> 05:28.081
What?

05:28.148 --> 05:29.353
Brian, your script...

05:30.895 --> 05:33.140
It was enchanting.

05:33.207 --> 05:34.982
This Brian Griffin--

05:35.051 --> 05:36.792
I've never met this
Brian Griffin.

05:36.860 --> 05:38.266
I would have told you
last night,

05:38.333 --> 05:40.679
but I was 100% sure
you ripped it off.

05:40.747 --> 05:42.522
But after spending
the last 18 hours

05:42.589 --> 05:43.862
on the Internet
and in libraries

05:43.930 --> 05:45.270
trying to find traces
of it somewhere,

05:45.338 --> 05:47.317
I couldn't find a thing.

05:47.385 --> 05:49.631
Well, I-I'm... I'm so happy
you liked it.

05:49.697 --> 05:51.138
Look, I don't want
to get your hopes up,

05:51.206 --> 05:53.617
but Daddy's got some
connections over at CBS,

05:53.684 --> 05:56.700
and I think he could
help you get this on TV.

05:56.768 --> 05:58.812
That's amazing,
Lois! Thank you!

05:58.880 --> 06:01.591
Oh, I am very proud of you.

06:01.660 --> 06:03.434
My God, a TV pilot.

06:03.503 --> 06:05.908
Wow, Brian, you must
feel like Hitler did

06:05.977 --> 06:08.019
<i>after he wrote Mein Kampf.</i>

06:08.088 --> 06:10.298
Well, we read it and
we don't like it.

06:10.367 --> 06:11.370
(dejected sigh)

06:11.438 --> 06:12.510
We love it!

06:12.576 --> 06:14.053
(both laughing)

06:14.121 --> 06:15.829
You got me!

06:15.898 --> 06:18.277
I could have killed you,
Mr. Weinberg!

06:29.838 --> 06:32.385
(chuckles)

06:32.454 --> 06:36.272
"Dear Diary, still no sign
of that tampon from last week,

06:36.339 --> 06:38.484
but the headaches
are getting worse."

06:38.552 --> 06:39.957
(gasps)

06:40.026 --> 06:42.370
Chris, what are you doing?!
Give me that!

06:42.439 --> 06:43.945
(laughing)

06:44.014 --> 06:45.889
(taunting laugh)

06:48.739 --> 06:51.180
Well, I'm off to buy
imaginary groceries.

06:58.080 --> 06:59.852
(gasps):
Do you think he's okay?

06:59.921 --> 07:01.261
I don't know.

07:01.330 --> 07:02.704
You idiot,
this is your fault.

07:02.771 --> 07:03.876
My fault?

07:03.943 --> 07:05.386
You were the one
chasing me.

07:05.453 --> 07:06.960
You were the one
reading my diary.

07:07.027 --> 07:08.065
LOIS:
Kids, breakfast.

07:08.132 --> 07:09.138
What do we do?

07:09.206 --> 07:10.242
Is he breathing?

07:10.310 --> 07:11.382
It-it looks like it.

07:11.448 --> 07:12.555
That's a good sign, right?

07:23.210 --> 07:26.427
Brian, I'm afraid I'm going
to have to be blunt with you.
Yeah?

07:26.495 --> 07:28.537
We love this pilot!

07:28.605 --> 07:32.054
Oh, my God, (laughs)
I felt like Hitler just then.

07:32.123 --> 07:33.799
What?
It was a joke.

07:33.867 --> 07:35.174
I just-I just said
I felt like Hitler.

07:36.917 --> 07:39.695
What?
Nobody here's Jewish, right?

07:39.763 --> 07:42.843
Uh, gee, I don't know,
are you Jewish, Gordon?

07:42.912 --> 07:44.920
Gee, I don't know,
Jeremy, are you?

07:44.988 --> 07:46.729
I-I had no idea.

07:46.798 --> 07:48.036
I'm-I'm sorry.

07:48.102 --> 07:50.749
Hey, Brian.
We're both Italian.

07:50.817 --> 07:52.625
(laughs)
You're in.

07:52.692 --> 07:53.998
(relieved sigh)

07:54.065 --> 07:55.338
(all laughing)

07:55.406 --> 07:57.114
That's a good one.
That's a good one.

07:57.183 --> 08:01.335
(chorus singing):

08:04.637 --> 08:05.808
Chris, I'm worried.

08:05.906 --> 08:07.211
Stewie's still
unconscious.

08:07.279 --> 08:08.517
Maybe he's just sleeping.

08:08.584 --> 08:09.859
Is Stewie in here?

08:09.926 --> 08:11.131
Ye-yes.

08:11.199 --> 08:12.437
Well, can you
give him to me?

08:12.505 --> 08:13.846
It's time
for his dinner.

08:13.912 --> 08:15.520
Oh, don't worry, Mom,
we'll bring him down.

08:15.589 --> 08:18.102
Anything I can do to make
your life a little easier.

08:18.170 --> 08:20.181
Well, you could always
grab the vacuum

08:20.250 --> 08:21.992
and clean up the
living room floor.

08:22.060 --> 08:23.667
Oh sure, I'll
take care of that.

08:23.736 --> 08:25.947
Are you, uh, running
off to your job?

08:26.016 --> 08:27.154
Oh.

08:27.222 --> 08:28.394
Yeah.

08:28.463 --> 08:30.103
Okay.
Yeah.

08:34.393 --> 08:36.805
Kids, what is taking so long?

08:36.874 --> 08:37.842
Dinner's ready.

08:41.830 --> 08:45.447
Oh, look at my little cowboy.

08:45.516 --> 08:48.231
Come on, Stewie, up
in your high chair.

08:50.209 --> 08:51.647
Boy, he must be starving.

08:51.715 --> 08:52.955
(nervous chuckle)

08:53.021 --> 08:54.729
Yeah, he's really
chowing down.

08:54.798 --> 08:56.406
Hey, how's the food,
Stewie?

08:56.474 --> 08:58.449
(cockney accent):
Oy, blimey,
it's right delicious,

08:58.517 --> 09:00.460
I do declare.
Wacka wacka.

09:00.529 --> 09:03.377
Brian, have you decided
who's gonna star in your show?

09:03.444 --> 09:04.951
Well, it's a tough role.

09:05.019 --> 09:07.097
I mean, Byron is a 25-year-old
single father

09:07.164 --> 09:08.737
going back to finish college

09:08.806 --> 09:10.582
so he can do right
by his four-year-old daughter.

09:10.650 --> 09:12.525
So it's got to be somebody
who can project

09:12.592 --> 09:14.903
a certain amount of
world-wizened sophistication,

09:14.972 --> 09:16.446
while at the same time retaining

09:16.513 --> 09:18.321
a certain youthfully
passionate idealism.

09:18.390 --> 09:20.132
You know who'd be
good? Big Jaws.

09:20.199 --> 09:21.806
Actually, in my mind,
there's only one actor

09:21.874 --> 09:24.151
who could do this role:
Elijah Wood.

09:24.218 --> 09:25.659
(with cockney accent):
Oh, Elijah Wood.

09:25.726 --> 09:28.506
I got a little bit
of a crush on him, I have.

09:28.575 --> 09:30.684
Oh, oh, look, Lois, wishbone.

09:30.753 --> 09:31.924
Okay, ready?

09:31.992 --> 09:34.303
One, two, three.

09:35.410 --> 09:36.348
Yes!

09:36.415 --> 09:37.286
I got five seconds.

09:42.716 --> 09:44.958
Hah! No gravity.

09:49.549 --> 09:51.661
There he is!

09:51.729 --> 09:52.801
Oh, go on.

09:52.869 --> 09:54.308
No, really, go on.

09:54.376 --> 09:56.252
(all laughing)

09:56.319 --> 09:58.399
Hi, I'm Todd Goldstein,

09:58.467 --> 10:00.748
senior executive of programming
here at the network.

10:00.816 --> 10:02.726
We are so happy
to be working with you.

10:02.794 --> 10:04.904
I hope you brought the magic
wand you wrote this with.

10:04.973 --> 10:06.445
Yep, it's right here.

10:06.514 --> 10:08.725
Watch, I'll turn this beautiful
princess into a toad.

10:08.792 --> 10:10.066
Bing.
Ribbit.

10:10.133 --> 10:11.675
(all laughing)

10:11.742 --> 10:12.948
Oh, all right,
I can tell

10:13.016 --> 10:14.656
this is gonna be
a fun process.

10:14.725 --> 10:17.040
You know, Brian, you've spun
gold into these pages,

10:17.109 --> 10:19.623
and it's our job to bring
that gold to the screen.

10:19.691 --> 10:22.207
So, what do you say we get this
casting session started, huh?

10:22.276 --> 10:23.885
Let's do it.
(all agreeing)

10:23.952 --> 10:26.967
All right!
Yeah!
(barking)

10:27.034 --> 10:29.212
Okay, now obviously,
we love Elijah Wood

10:29.279 --> 10:31.624
for the part of Byron
as much as you do.

10:31.692 --> 10:33.296
The audition
is just a formality.

10:33.365 --> 10:34.469
Let's bring him in.

10:34.537 --> 10:36.143
Elijah?

10:36.211 --> 10:37.383
Hi, everyone.

10:37.452 --> 10:40.131
Hey, Elijah,
how are you?
How you doing?

10:40.200 --> 10:41.504
So should I just
jump into this?

10:41.572 --> 10:43.247
Whenever you're ready.

10:43.315 --> 10:44.955
Okay, I'll read
you in, Elijah.

10:45.023 --> 10:47.066
Now, this is the scene where
Byron's professor fails him

10:47.133 --> 10:48.607
for missing the
chemistry midterm

10:48.674 --> 10:50.985
to take his daughter
to the doctor.

10:51.054 --> 10:54.402
I'm sorry, Byron, missing the
midterm is an automatic failure.

10:54.470 --> 10:56.782
Go ahead, Professor Watkins,
fail me if you want.

10:56.850 --> 10:58.692
Give me an "F" on the exam.

10:58.760 --> 11:01.071
I don't care because
I got an "A" today...

11:01.138 --> 11:02.546
as a dad.

11:02.612 --> 11:04.019
Maybe this is news to you,

11:04.087 --> 11:06.399
but love isn't some element
on your periodic table.

11:06.466 --> 11:07.671
So, you know what?

11:07.739 --> 11:09.714
Keep your chromium
and magnesium,

11:09.782 --> 11:12.361
because I discovered
a much more precious element.

11:12.430 --> 11:13.972
I discovered Dadmium.

11:15.181 --> 11:16.922
Wow.

11:16.991 --> 11:19.437
Does that audition come
with a box of tissues?

11:19.505 --> 11:20.677
That was a
great read.

11:20.745 --> 11:23.123
Thanks, everybody.

11:23.192 --> 11:24.731
I mean, come on.
How great is he?

11:24.799 --> 11:27.377
Wow. He's amazing.
That's our guy.

11:27.445 --> 11:28.651
That's our Byron.

11:28.718 --> 11:29.858
Great. Then we're
good to go.

11:29.925 --> 11:31.899
Look, since we're playing
with house money,

11:31.967 --> 11:34.413
we do have
one more guy.

11:34.481 --> 11:35.853
What?
Oh, don't worry.

11:35.922 --> 11:36.927
He's not Byron.

11:36.995 --> 11:38.569
Somebody upstairs
threw out a name,

11:38.638 --> 11:39.608
got real excited
about it,

11:39.676 --> 11:41.015
so we're just
playing the game.

11:41.083 --> 11:43.228
Okay, so who?

11:43.294 --> 11:45.506
Hey, what's up, douche bags?

11:45.572 --> 11:47.247
I'm here to audition
for this stupid pilot.

11:47.315 --> 11:49.458
(all laughing)

11:49.525 --> 11:50.699
James Woods?

11:50.767 --> 11:52.542
Thanks for
coming in, Jimmy.

11:52.609 --> 11:53.881
Do you need
a copy of the scene?

11:53.950 --> 11:55.424
Nah, I got it memorized.

11:55.492 --> 11:56.730
Just like
your wife's phone number.

11:56.798 --> 11:58.707
(all laughing)

11:58.775 --> 11:59.982
No, he didn't!

12:00.049 --> 12:01.522
I do, though.
I have it memorized.

12:01.591 --> 12:02.728
Wait a second. I
don't want this guy

12:02.796 --> 12:03.935
anywhere near
this show!

12:04.004 --> 12:05.678
He's been harassing
my family for years!

12:05.745 --> 12:08.156
He stalked my best friend and
then he stole his identity!

12:08.224 --> 12:10.200
(all laughing)

12:10.268 --> 12:12.009
Oh, my God.
That's an episode!

12:12.078 --> 12:13.384
Brian, will you
read him in?

12:13.453 --> 12:15.495
(sighs)

12:15.563 --> 12:16.969
I'm sorry, Byron.

12:17.038 --> 12:19.247
Missing the midterm
is an automatic failure.

12:19.316 --> 12:21.459
Hey, science face,
I got an "F" for you.

12:21.527 --> 12:22.901
It stands for "(bleep) you."

12:22.968 --> 12:24.208
(all laughing)

12:24.275 --> 12:25.446
There he is!

12:25.514 --> 12:26.620
Hey, and guess what?

12:26.687 --> 12:27.826
I discovered a new element.

12:27.894 --> 12:28.998
(farts)

12:29.067 --> 12:31.411
(all laughing)

12:31.480 --> 12:33.791
Okay, thanks a lot,
everybody. I got to go.

12:33.857 --> 12:35.701
I got some school
bus seats to sniff.

12:35.769 --> 12:36.873
(all laughing)

12:36.942 --> 12:38.449
Seriously,
stop it!

12:38.517 --> 12:40.259
Okay, did everyone else
feel that?

12:40.326 --> 12:41.230
I felt it.

12:41.297 --> 12:42.770
Oh, I felt it, too.

12:42.838 --> 12:44.579
I felt whatever the most senior
executive in this room felt.

12:44.648 --> 12:46.054
Okay, good.
So we all felt it.

12:46.123 --> 12:48.030
Wait, you're not seriously
considering him, are you?

12:48.099 --> 12:49.941
James Woods is insane.

12:50.009 --> 12:51.584
And on top of that,
he's, like, 60.

12:51.651 --> 12:53.527
The character of Byron
is 25 years old.

12:53.595 --> 12:54.700
I know, I know.

12:54.768 --> 12:57.281
But what if he wasn't?

12:57.349 --> 12:58.385
What? But he is.

12:58.454 --> 12:59.490
...n't.

12:59.558 --> 13:01.000
My god, this
is a nightmare.

13:01.069 --> 13:02.910
I thought you said you weren't
gonna mess with my show.

13:02.977 --> 13:04.418
It's a small
change, Brian.

13:04.486 --> 13:05.758
You won't
even notice it.

13:05.826 --> 13:07.366
You guys aren't gonna
(bleep) believe this.

13:07.434 --> 13:08.607
Call a tow truck.

13:08.674 --> 13:10.884
I just banged
into some homo's Prius.

13:13.600 --> 13:15.977
This is Rob, our director.

13:16.046 --> 13:18.924
Hey, Brian. Really looking
forward to making your show.

13:18.991 --> 13:21.469
What's, uh, wha-what's
with all those empty seats?

13:21.537 --> 13:23.080
Well, that's where the audience
is gonna sit.

13:23.147 --> 13:24.419
Audience?

13:24.488 --> 13:25.927
This isn't a sitcom.
It's a drama.

13:25.994 --> 13:29.377
Well, we thought it would be a
lot funnier if it was a sitcom.

13:29.446 --> 13:31.120
It's-It's-It's not supposed
to be funny.

13:31.188 --> 13:33.231
It's a serious drama
about a single father

13:33.299 --> 13:34.739
raising his
four-year-old daughter.

13:34.807 --> 13:36.682
Oh, right. That's
the other thing.

13:36.749 --> 13:38.859
Now, this is just a thought,
but we're doing it.

13:38.928 --> 13:40.300
She's 18.
What?

13:40.368 --> 13:42.041
It was James Woods' idea

13:42.110 --> 13:43.751
and we thought it was
just the right hook.

13:43.819 --> 13:45.327
I mean, how
great is that?

13:45.396 --> 13:48.578
A father and a daughter,
both in college together?

13:48.647 --> 13:50.656
Hey, do you guys think this
outfit is too revealing?

13:50.724 --> 13:51.929
What-what do
you think, Brian?

13:51.998 --> 13:53.001
I mean, what-what
do you think

13:53.068 --> 13:54.309
of the ta-tas,
you know what I mean?

13:54.376 --> 13:56.553
Can we do better? I think
we can do better, right?

13:56.620 --> 13:58.196
Who is this? Where'd
you get this girl?

13:58.264 --> 14:00.339
Oh, by the way,
I need a chimp sidekick.

14:04.797 --> 14:06.404
Maybe we should take him
to the hospital.

14:06.472 --> 14:07.877
I think the maggots
have evolved

14:07.945 --> 14:09.821
to the point that they've
developed space travel.

14:09.890 --> 14:11.730
(rocket booming)

14:13.843 --> 14:15.887
We just gotta clean
the wound, Meg.

14:15.953 --> 14:17.393
Hand me that
scrub brush.

14:18.668 --> 14:19.706
Hey, Chris.

14:19.775 --> 14:21.615
Hey-Hey, Dad.

14:23.459 --> 14:26.340
So, question: How long has
Stewie been unconscious?

14:26.408 --> 14:27.680
Oh my God,
Chris, he knows!

14:27.748 --> 14:30.194
Dad, I am so sorry.

14:30.261 --> 14:32.640
We should have told somebody,
but we were too scared,

14:32.707 --> 14:35.118
and-and-and I wanted
to take him to the hospital,

14:35.186 --> 14:36.158
but Chris wouldn't let me.

14:36.226 --> 14:37.330
Good, Chris.

14:37.398 --> 14:38.537
I've taught you well.

14:38.604 --> 14:39.709
You've got
the right instincts.

14:39.778 --> 14:41.083
When you were babies,

14:41.151 --> 14:42.557
I used to knock you kids out
every month or so,

14:42.625 --> 14:45.105
sometimes by accident, sometimes
when the Patriots lost.

14:45.171 --> 14:47.418
You just gotta cover it up,
and eventually it all works out.

14:47.484 --> 14:48.992
Meg, this is
a list of hats.

14:49.059 --> 14:50.130
I need these
by 4:00.

14:50.197 --> 14:51.704
Good idea.

14:51.772 --> 14:53.648
One of those hats would have
helped him at the park today.

14:53.717 --> 14:56.833
Chris, did you put a
coonskin cap on Stewie?

14:56.901 --> 14:57.971
CHRIS:
No, why?

14:59.681 --> 15:00.987
(laughing)

15:01.054 --> 15:03.464
They look like
little robbers.

15:07.687 --> 15:09.261
Hey, good evening, everyone,

15:09.330 --> 15:12.410
and welcome to the first taping
of CBS's new hit comedy,

15:12.478 --> 15:13.717
<i>Class Holes!</i>

15:13.785 --> 15:15.428
(cheers and applause)

15:15.495 --> 15:17.337
Wait a minute.
That's not the title.

15:17.404 --> 15:19.246
It's called "What I Learned
on Jefferson Street."

15:19.315 --> 15:21.390
Yeah, it didn't make
a lot of sense

15:21.459 --> 15:22.765
now that he lives
on Boner Street.

15:22.834 --> 15:24.475
And... action!

15:24.542 --> 15:26.821
Okay, Hillary,
you know the bet.

15:26.889 --> 15:28.696
You have to sleep
with the next guy

15:28.765 --> 15:29.971
that walks
through that door.

15:30.039 --> 15:31.378
Got it.

15:31.445 --> 15:32.551
Dad?!

15:32.619 --> 15:34.393
(canned laughter)

15:34.461 --> 15:35.599
Hillary?

15:35.667 --> 15:36.705
(canned laughter)

15:36.773 --> 15:37.910
You're in college?

15:37.979 --> 15:39.653
(canned laughter)

15:39.721 --> 15:41.697
You're my new
college roommate?!

15:41.765 --> 15:43.204
(canned laughter)

15:43.273 --> 15:44.747
We're gonna be
living together?

15:44.815 --> 15:46.087
(canned laughter)

15:46.156 --> 15:47.663
In the same dorm?

15:47.730 --> 15:48.969
(canned laughter)

15:49.037 --> 15:50.377
For the whole year?!

15:50.445 --> 15:51.817
(canned laughter)

15:51.886 --> 15:53.761
With my chimp sidekick?!

15:53.829 --> 15:55.033
(cheering)

15:55.102 --> 15:57.446
Brian, they love it.

15:57.514 --> 15:59.291
Wha-what is this?
This isn't my script.

15:59.358 --> 16:00.597
What the hell'd
you do to it?!

16:00.665 --> 16:02.306
And I've got
great news for you.

16:02.373 --> 16:04.651
We just got an e-mail
from the network heads.

16:04.720 --> 16:07.199
<i>They want to pick up
Class Holes! for 22 episodes!</i>

16:07.267 --> 16:08.840
Really?
Yeah, aren't
you thrilled?

16:08.909 --> 16:11.924
I, uh...
I'm not sure.

16:11.992 --> 16:14.972
Hey, guys, the chimp just ripped
the other girl's boob off.

16:15.041 --> 16:17.017
Please tell me
you got that on camera.

16:21.932 --> 16:23.908
Lois, I-I wish you hadn't
gone through all this trouble.

16:23.976 --> 16:25.082
Are you kidding?

16:25.150 --> 16:26.690
I'm so excited.

16:26.758 --> 16:28.464
From the first time
I read that script,

16:28.533 --> 16:30.476
I just knew it was
gonna make it on TV.

16:30.545 --> 16:31.683
I could see it.

16:31.750 --> 16:33.257
Well, yeah, I mean,

16:33.324 --> 16:36.775
but the real victory is just
having a show make it to TV.

16:36.844 --> 16:38.787
I mean, we almost don't
even need to watch.

16:38.854 --> 16:40.898
<i>Hey, what's the cast of
Two and a Half Men doing here?</i>

16:40.967 --> 16:43.011
Well, you're always
saying our show sucks.

16:43.079 --> 16:44.182
Let's see yours.

16:44.251 --> 16:46.294
Come on, Peter!
Come on, kids!

16:46.362 --> 16:47.870
The show's about to start!

16:47.939 --> 16:50.217
I'm coming.
I was just gettin' Stewie.

16:50.285 --> 16:52.262
All right, got the little
guy up from his nap.

16:52.330 --> 16:55.408
Say hello to everyone, Stewie.

16:55.475 --> 16:57.081
ALL:
Aww.

16:57.149 --> 16:58.155
Oh, oh, look,
everybody.

16:58.223 --> 17:00.434
It's starting.

17:02.678 --> 17:04.219
в™Є There was a man в™Є

17:04.287 --> 17:05.962
в™Є He dropped out в™Є

17:06.029 --> 17:07.635
в™Є Now he's back в™Є

17:07.703 --> 17:09.980
в™Є He had a daughter в™Є

17:10.048 --> 17:11.421
в™Є Now she's in college
with him. в™Є

17:11.489 --> 17:14.671
What a pain in my class!

17:23.884 --> 17:25.760
(canned laughter on TV)

17:28.041 --> 17:30.250
Well, Dad,
I owe you an apology.

17:30.317 --> 17:32.728
You said you'd win that
talent show and you did.

17:32.797 --> 17:34.773
Now everyone's doing
the Charleston.

17:34.841 --> 17:35.945
(canned laughter)

17:36.013 --> 17:37.118
Not everyone.

17:37.186 --> 17:38.794
Someone's doing
The Monkey.

17:38.861 --> 17:39.966
(music playing)

17:40.033 --> 17:41.842
BOTH:
Mr. Nubbins.

17:41.909 --> 17:44.022
Well, if you
can't beat 'em...

17:44.090 --> 17:45.967
(music playing)

17:48.180 --> 17:50.925
(turns off TV)

17:50.994 --> 17:52.936
So, uh...
what'd you guys think?

17:53.005 --> 17:55.049
Not the worst thing
in the world, right?

17:56.456 --> 17:57.828
Oh, ha-ha.

17:57.897 --> 17:59.672
Time for bed,
Stewie.

18:02.387 --> 18:04.933
So, really, everybody,
what-what'd you think?

18:05.000 --> 18:06.405
You want to know
what I think?

18:06.473 --> 18:07.645
Well, yeah.

18:07.712 --> 18:08.815
You son of a bitch!

18:08.884 --> 18:09.988
Oh! Oh my God!

18:10.057 --> 18:11.095
It's a travesty!
Joe, stop it!

18:11.161 --> 18:12.300
And I will kill you!

18:12.368 --> 18:13.572
Okay, okay.
Come on, Joe.

18:13.640 --> 18:15.248
You bastard,
we hired a sitter for this!

18:15.316 --> 18:16.490
Come on,
come on, Joe!
Okay, come on.

18:16.556 --> 18:18.834
Guys, keep his anus
above his head!

18:18.903 --> 18:20.209
Okay, okay.
All right, all right.

18:20.276 --> 18:21.113
Okay, careful.
Careful, Peter.

18:21.181 --> 18:22.186
(two gunshots)

18:22.255 --> 18:23.828
PETER:
Oh, boy. Oh, boy.

18:23.895 --> 18:25.704
Look, I'm sure you're
disappointed, Lois,

18:25.772 --> 18:27.010
but it wasn't my fault.

18:27.077 --> 18:28.820
There-there were so
many people interfering.

18:28.887 --> 18:30.159
Of course
it's gonna be bad.

18:30.227 --> 18:32.604
I'm not disappointed
because it was bad.

18:32.673 --> 18:35.218
I'm disappointed
because you sold out.

18:35.287 --> 18:37.329
You had something really
special there, Brian.

18:37.398 --> 18:39.542
You had a vision and
you let them change it

18:39.610 --> 18:41.216
because you didn't
believe in yourself.

18:41.284 --> 18:42.959
Lois, what was I gonna do?

18:43.027 --> 18:44.969
It was either do it their way
or get cancelled.

18:45.038 --> 18:48.052
Well, at least then you would
have had your integrity.

18:55.490 --> 18:56.897
You guys wanted
to see me?

18:56.965 --> 18:59.510
Look, Brian, we're really
excited about the ratings.

18:59.578 --> 19:01.655
We're just really disappointed
with how low they are.

19:01.722 --> 19:03.364
We did some testing
this morning,

19:03.432 --> 19:04.872
and we think we've
discovered the problem.

19:04.939 --> 19:08.390
People want something
more real, more grounded.

19:08.457 --> 19:10.332
That's exactly
what I think, too.

19:10.400 --> 19:12.477
We need to get back
to the original script.

19:12.545 --> 19:14.789
Byron wants to do right
by his daughter,

19:14.857 --> 19:16.833
and I think that everyone
can relate to that.

19:16.901 --> 19:18.474
That's what America
wants to see.

19:18.543 --> 19:20.686
The simple love within a family

19:20.752 --> 19:22.831
that conquers all
and makes life worth living.

19:22.898 --> 19:26.651
What if I'm a cop and the chimp
is my reincarnated partner?

19:26.720 --> 19:27.790
Yes!

19:27.858 --> 19:29.031
That's perfect!

19:29.097 --> 19:30.806
See? That's relatable!

19:30.874 --> 19:32.346
How the hell is that relatable?

19:32.415 --> 19:34.291
And every month,
he's got a story

19:34.359 --> 19:35.999
for why he doesn't have
his half of the rent.

19:36.067 --> 19:38.043
I'm not doing this!

19:38.111 --> 19:39.952
What do you mean,
you're not doing it?

19:40.021 --> 19:41.596
I'm drawing the line.

19:41.664 --> 19:43.473
I wrote a script that really
meant a lot to me,

19:43.541 --> 19:44.913
and you guys took it

19:44.981 --> 19:46.723
and turned it into something
cheap and ridiculous.

19:46.789 --> 19:47.963
I've had enough.

19:48.032 --> 19:49.806
I'm not selling out anymore.
I quit.

19:51.718 --> 19:53.860
Do you guys have a
studio lawyer or something,

19:53.927 --> 19:55.335
'cause I banged
that chick,

19:55.403 --> 19:57.847
and I think she is
well under 18 years old.

20:00.899 --> 20:02.237
All right, he's
not waking up.

20:02.305 --> 20:03.778
I think we gotta take
these bandages off

20:03.847 --> 20:05.419
and see what we're
dealin' with here.

20:05.488 --> 20:07.869
(flies buzzing)

20:07.936 --> 20:09.611
Oh, crap, you
smell that?

20:09.679 --> 20:10.683
That's brain smell.

20:10.751 --> 20:12.325
Okay, I don't care
what you say.

20:12.393 --> 20:14.032
He's really hurt,
and I'm gonna do

20:14.101 --> 20:15.543
what I should have done
in the first place.

20:15.611 --> 20:16.916
I'm taking him
to the hospital.

20:16.985 --> 20:19.730
No, no. I got one
last plan, Meg.

20:28.439 --> 20:29.577
Oh, my God!

20:29.645 --> 20:31.118
Stewie!

20:31.186 --> 20:33.531
Oh, God.
What have I done?!

20:33.600 --> 20:35.406
Looks like you
ran over him, Lois.

20:35.474 --> 20:38.287
Oh, no!
He's unconscious!

20:38.354 --> 20:40.229
Peter, Peter, we have
to cover this up!

20:40.298 --> 20:41.837
Yeah, but... What?!

20:41.906 --> 20:43.915
Let's put a hat on him
to cover the wound,

20:43.983 --> 20:45.188
and-and then let's
get some makeup

20:45.256 --> 20:46.731
and draw eyeballs
on his eyelids

20:46.798 --> 20:49.074
and find a way to pin
this on someone else.

20:49.142 --> 20:51.755
I love you
so much right now.

20:51.823 --> 20:53.062
Let's go to the hospital.

20:54.393 --> 20:59.315
-- Sync by <font color=#00FF00>elderman</font> --
-- for <font color=#00FFFF>www.addic7ed.com</font> --

21:02.110 --> 21:04.386
Well, Brian, I'm
proud of you.

21:04.455 --> 21:04.957
You went in there and you stood
up for what you believed in.

21:06.322 --> 21:08.197
Yeah, now I don't have
a TV Show anymore.

21:08.510 --> 21:10.338
Oh who cares about that.

21:10.457 --> 21:12.801
You made the right choice
for your own integrity

21:12.869 --> 21:14.209
And that's what matters.

21:14.277 --> 21:15.582
Well, I suppose
you're right.

21:15.650 --> 21:18.063
And hey, at least I got
something on the air.

21:18.131 --> 21:19.670
I mean, not everyone
can say that.

21:19.738 --> 21:21.320
(clears throat)

21:21.389 --> 21:23.960
<i>Announcer (on tv):
We now return to big jaws.</i>

21:24.028 --> 21:27.852
Aah! Stop eating
our boat, jaws!

21:27.920 --> 21:29.567
Rrr. I'm gonna
eat your boat,

21:29.635 --> 21:31.120
And then I'm gonna
eat you guys!

21:31.187 --> 21:33.024
Rarr!

21:33.090 --> 21:34.273
Oh, my god, what's that?

21:34.341 --> 21:35.663
It's bigger jaws!

21:35.732 --> 21:36.847
Oh, my god!

21:36.915 --> 21:38.132
Now we have
a common enemy.

21:38.200 --> 21:39.281
We have
to work together.

21:39.349 --> 21:40.841
I already got
a sequel in mind.

21:40.909 --> 21:42.499
<i>It's called
way bigger jaws.</i>

21:42.567 --> 21:44.932
Hey, I just found
out it's November!

21:45.000 --> 21:47.430
What the (bleep)
happened?!

21:47.431 --> 22:48.431
Thank you for watching «Family Guy» on
www.FOX-fan.ru